1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,466
Stand eight metres from a wall.

2
00:00:02,466 --> 00:00:03,764
Walk half the way.

3
00:00:03,764 --> 00:00:05,790
Now walk half of what is left.

4
00:00:05,790 --> 00:00:07,078
Again, and again.

5
00:00:07,478 --> 00:00:08,690
Four metres left.

6
00:00:08,690 --> 00:00:09,590
Two.

7
00:00:09,173 --> 00:00:10,073
One.

8
00:00:09,656 --> 00:00:10,679
Half a metre.

9
00:00:10,679 --> 00:00:11,579
A quarter.

10
00:00:11,952 --> 00:00:14,272
Diya tried this game in
the college corridor.

11
00:00:14,622 --> 00:00:17,775
After twenty moves the gap
is thinner than a hair.

12
00:00:17,775 --> 00:00:19,761
But there is always half left.

13
00:00:19,761 --> 00:00:21,701
Does she ever touch the wall?

14
00:00:22,201 --> 00:00:26,717
She never lands on it, yet the gap
gets as close to zero as you like.

15
00:00:26,717 --> 00:00:28,235
That idea has a name.

16
00:00:28,235 --> 00:00:29,754
It is called a limit.

17
00:00:30,404 --> 00:00:31,764
So what is a limit?

18
00:00:32,114 --> 00:00:33,849
A limit asks one question.

19
00:00:33,849 --> 00:00:38,106
When the input gets closer and closer
to a number, what does the output

20
00:00:38,106 --> 00:00:39,119
get close to?

21
00:00:39,519 --> 00:00:41,186
We write it like this.

22
00:00:41,186 --> 00:00:44,954
The limit, as x tends to
a, of f of x, equals l.

23
00:00:45,354 --> 00:00:49,220
x tends to a means x creeps
towards a, as close as we like.

24
00:00:49,220 --> 00:00:51,106
But x never has to equal a.

25
00:00:51,506 --> 00:00:54,393
And l is the value that
f of x settles near.

26
00:00:54,393 --> 00:00:58,033
For Diya, the moves keep going
and the gap tends to zero.

27
00:00:58,683 --> 00:01:01,239
Now watch a real function do this.

28
00:01:01,239 --> 00:01:05,584
Take h of x equals x squared
minus four, over x minus two.

29
00:01:05,934 --> 00:01:07,418
Try x equals two.

30
00:01:07,418 --> 00:01:10,453
The top is zero and the bottom is zero.

31
00:01:10,453 --> 00:01:14,806
Zero over zero has no value,
so h of two does not exist.

32
00:01:15,206 --> 00:01:17,242
So we creep up on two instead.

33
00:01:17,242 --> 00:01:21,691
From the left: one point nine, then
one point nine nine, then one point

34
00:01:21,691 --> 00:01:22,798
nine nine nine.

35
00:01:23,198 --> 00:01:27,643
h gives three point nine, then three
point nine nine, then three point

36
00:01:27,643 --> 00:01:28,801
nine nine nine.

37
00:01:28,801 --> 00:01:30,627
It is closing in on four.

38
00:01:31,027 --> 00:01:35,775
From the right: two point one, two
point zero one, two point zero zero

39
00:01:35,775 --> 00:01:36,675
one.

40
00:01:36,291 --> 00:01:41,141
h gives four point one, four point
zero one, four point zero zero one.

41
00:01:41,141 --> 00:01:42,912
Also closing in on four.

42
00:01:43,562 --> 00:01:44,462
Why four?

43
00:01:44,380 --> 00:01:46,895
Here is the reason, in small steps.

44
00:01:47,245 --> 00:01:48,145
Step one.

45
00:01:48,140 --> 00:01:53,710
The top, x squared minus four, splits
into x minus two, times x plus two.

46
00:01:54,460 --> 00:01:55,360
Step two.

47
00:01:55,346 --> 00:01:58,817
While x is not two, x
minus two is not zero.

48
00:01:58,817 --> 00:02:01,661
So we can cancel it, top and bottom.

49
00:02:02,411 --> 00:02:03,311
Step three.

50
00:02:03,235 --> 00:02:05,320
What is left is just x plus two.

51
00:02:05,320 --> 00:02:08,550
A straight line, with one
tiny hole at x equals two.

52
00:02:09,300 --> 00:02:10,208
Step four.

53
00:02:10,208 --> 00:02:13,556
As x tends to two, x
plus two tends to four.

54
00:02:13,556 --> 00:02:17,760
So the limit is four, even
though h of two does not exist.

55
00:02:18,410 --> 00:02:19,399
Side by side.

56
00:02:19,399 --> 00:02:23,606
The value at a point is what the
function gives exactly at that point.

57
00:02:23,606 --> 00:02:25,063
Here, there is none.

58
00:02:25,463 --> 00:02:29,082
The limit is what the function
gets close to near that point.

59
00:02:29,082 --> 00:02:30,379
Here, it is four.

60
00:02:30,379 --> 00:02:32,612
A limit looks near a, never at a.

61
00:02:33,212 --> 00:02:34,203
Now you try.

62
00:02:34,203 --> 00:02:38,636
Can a function have a limit at a
point where it has no value at all?

63
00:02:38,636 --> 00:02:39,866
Pause and decide.

64
00:02:43,066 --> 00:02:43,966
Yes!

65
00:02:43,536 --> 00:02:46,866
h has no value at two, but
its limit there is four.

66
00:02:46,866 --> 00:02:49,000
The hole does not stop the limit.

67
00:02:49,650 --> 00:02:51,627
Notice we came from two sides.

68
00:02:51,627 --> 00:02:55,245
Coming from numbers just below
a gives the left hand limit.

69
00:02:55,645 --> 00:02:59,206
Coming from numbers just above
a gives the right hand limit.

70
00:02:59,606 --> 00:03:03,254
The rule from NCERT: the limit exists
only when the left hand limit and

71
00:03:03,254 --> 00:03:05,487
the right hand limit are the same number.

72
00:03:06,087 --> 00:03:06,987
Test it.

73
00:03:06,869 --> 00:03:11,299
The greatest integer function,
square bracket x, at x equals one.

74
00:03:11,299 --> 00:03:12,864
Does the limit exist?

75
00:03:12,864 --> 00:03:14,114
Pause and think.

76
00:03:17,314 --> 00:03:18,214
No!

77
00:03:17,753 --> 00:03:21,533
Just below one, like zero
point nine nine, it gives zero.

78
00:03:21,533 --> 00:03:23,596
Just above one, it gives one.

79
00:03:23,596 --> 00:03:26,851
Zero and one do not match,
so there is no limit.

80
00:03:27,501 --> 00:03:28,514
Remember this.

81
00:03:28,514 --> 00:03:33,191
A limit looks near the point, from
both sides, and both sides must agree.

82
00:03:33,891 --> 00:03:35,581
Now, one worked example.

83
00:03:35,931 --> 00:03:40,943
f of x is x squared for x less than
two, and three x minus two for x more

84
00:03:40,943 --> 00:03:41,843
than two.

85
00:03:41,775 --> 00:03:43,511
And f of two is seven.

86
00:03:43,511 --> 00:03:45,926
Find the limit as x tends to two.

87
00:03:46,426 --> 00:03:47,326
Step one.

88
00:03:47,312 --> 00:03:50,165
From the left, f of x is x squared.

89
00:03:50,165 --> 00:03:53,072
As x tends to two, it tends to four.

90
00:03:53,822 --> 00:03:54,722
Step two.

91
00:03:54,704 --> 00:03:58,158
From the right, f of x
is three x minus two.

92
00:03:58,158 --> 00:04:02,160
As x tends to two, it tends
to six minus two, four.

93
00:04:02,910 --> 00:04:03,810
Step three.

94
00:04:03,658 --> 00:04:04,912
Both sides give four.

95
00:04:04,912 --> 00:04:07,215
So the limit exists, and it equals four.

96
00:04:07,965 --> 00:04:08,865
Step four.

97
00:04:08,773 --> 00:04:11,698
f of two is seven, but
that does not matter.

98
00:04:11,698 --> 00:04:14,326
The limit never looks at the point itself.

99
00:04:15,076 --> 00:04:18,179
So the limit is four,
and the value is seven.

100
00:04:18,179 --> 00:04:19,720
They can be different.

101
00:04:20,370 --> 00:04:21,834
Now, the exam trap.

102
00:04:22,184 --> 00:04:26,045
Find the limit of mod x
over x, as x tends to zero.

103
00:04:26,045 --> 00:04:30,756
Many students try x equals zero point
one and zero point zero one, get

104
00:04:30,756 --> 00:04:33,023
one each time, and write one.

105
00:04:33,423 --> 00:04:35,608
But they only checked one side.

106
00:04:35,608 --> 00:04:38,644
From the left, mod x over x is minus one.

107
00:04:38,644 --> 00:04:42,822
Minus one and one do not match,
so the limit does not exist.

108
00:04:43,472 --> 00:04:44,884
Where is this asked?

109
00:04:44,884 --> 00:04:49,271
In JEE Main, as functions defined
in pieces, or with square bracket x,

110
00:04:49,271 --> 00:04:51,763
where you check both sides of a point.

111
00:04:52,363 --> 00:04:53,477
Exam level now.

112
00:04:53,477 --> 00:04:56,102
If the limit exists, what is its value?

113
00:04:56,102 --> 00:04:57,264
Pause and solve.

114
00:05:02,664 --> 00:05:04,258
The answer is B.

115
00:05:04,258 --> 00:05:05,158
Five.

116
00:05:05,451 --> 00:05:08,729
From the left, a x plus three
tends to two a plus three.

117
00:05:09,229 --> 00:05:12,293
From the right, x squared
plus a tends to four plus a.

118
00:05:12,793 --> 00:05:16,157
For the limit to exist,
both sides must match.

119
00:05:16,157 --> 00:05:19,870
Two a plus three equals
four plus a, so a is one.

120
00:05:19,870 --> 00:05:23,350
Then the limit is four
plus one, which is five.

121
00:05:23,950 --> 00:05:25,490
Here is your recap.

122
00:05:25,490 --> 00:05:27,073
Screenshot this card.

123
00:05:30,273 --> 00:05:32,459
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

124
00:05:32,859 --> 00:05:33,759
One.

125
00:05:33,336 --> 00:05:36,280
What does x tends to a
mean, in plain words?

126
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:38,080
Two.

127
00:05:37,673 --> 00:05:39,423
h of two does not exist.

128
00:05:39,423 --> 00:05:42,584
So why is the limit of
h at two equal to four?

129
00:05:43,484 --> 00:05:44,384
Three.

130
00:05:43,990 --> 00:05:45,875
When does a limit fail to exist?

131
00:05:46,975 --> 00:05:50,454
Next time: a big limit full of
sums, products and fractions.

132
00:05:50,454 --> 00:05:52,419
Can you break it into small ones?

133
00:05:52,419 --> 00:05:54,603
We find out in the algebra of limits.
